{
  "title": "OpenMed \u2014 Healthcare / Clinical NLP (1,248\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: OpenMed\nDomain: Healthcare / Clinical NLP | Scale: medium | Stars: 1,248\nProblem: Clinical and biomedical text contains rich information that is difficult to extract automatically; OpenMed provides a production-ready medical NLP toolkit for entity extraction, PII de-identification, and medical reasoning without vendor lock-in.\nTarget users: Healthcare software developers, Clinical NLP researchers, Healthcare IT teams building HIPAA-compliant systems, Data scientists working with electronic health records, iOS/macOS app developers building on-device medical AI\nCore features: Medical Named Entity Recognition (NER) with 12+ curated specialized models; HIPAA-compliant PII detection and de-identification (18 Safe Harbor identifiers); REST API service via FastAPI for backend integration; Batch processing for multi-document workflows with progress tracking; On-device inference via MLX (Apple Silicon) and CoreML for Swift/iOS/macOS; Configuration profiles (dev/prod/test/fast) and performance profiling tools; Offline/air-gapped deployment from local model directories; Zero-shot NER via GLiNER-family models\nIntegrations: Hugging Face Hub (model hosting and download), Hugging Face Transformers, MLX (Apple Silicon runtime), CoreML (Apple on-device inference), GLiNER (zero-shot NER), Docker\nTech stack: Python (FastAPI + Uvicorn) + Docker, Apple MLX/CoreML (on-device)\nKey libraries: transformers, huggingface-hub, accelerate, tokenizers, fastapi, uvicorn, mlx, coremltools, gliner, torch, safetensors, pysbd, faker\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Dual-runtime design (Python/HF Transformers + Swift/MLX-CoreML) enables both server-side and fully on-device sovereign AI deployments from a single model registry.\n- Optional dependency extras (hf, mlx, gliner, coreml, service) allow a lean core install with heavy ML dependencies only pulled in when needed, reducing footprint for constrained environments.\n- HIPAA compliance is treated as a first-class feature with dedicated PII extraction and de-identification endpoints, signalling that regulatory requirements shaped the API surface.\n- Support for local/air-gapped model paths alongside Hugging Face Hub lookups reflects a deliberate design for healthcare environments where internet access to external services may be restricted.",
